"The patient underwent a robot-assisted radical prostatectomy with a pathological stage of pT2, negative surgical margins, and no invasion of the seminal vesicles. Absence of lymph nodes removed is noted, which widens uncertainty regarding nodal status. Biopsy results showed a mixed pattern, with one session yielding Gleason 0+0 and a subsequent session showing Gleason 3+4 in the left peripheral zone. The preoperative PSA was elevated at 8.4. The favorable surgical findings, including negative margins and no seminal vesicle invasion, are positive prognostic factors. Countervailing factors include the higher preoperative PSA and the presence of Gleason 3+4 histology identified during biopsy. Missing postoperative PSA or follow-up data prevents a definitive assessment of the subsequent trajectory. Information regarding postoperative PSA levels and its trend would significantly revise the prognosis. The current heuristic horizon suggests a range for recurrence without an event. The fixed estimated horizon is 87.65 months. This represents expected follow-up without an event, not a known recurrence date. The sensitivity range is 56.97–118.32 months; its coverage has not been validated."
